 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "Kg8mYmFjBwAJ",
   "from": "Ana Catarina Silva",
   "author": "fa81d557c3e3d3b7",
   "date": "2019-02-26T09:47:01Z",
   "subject": "Plotting clade names in circular tree",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:small\">Hi,</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">I am currently trying to plot several clade names in a circular tree with something like this: </div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div><br></div><div>&gt; head(cladesData)</div><div>clades  node     col</div><div>capensis   5497 #000000</div><div>hemprichii 11453 #FF7F00</div><div>lemniscatus  7407 #7FC97F</div><div>patagonicus 11563 #666666</div><div>gambianus  7666 #1F78B4</div><div>argenteus 11811 #BF5B17</div></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div> p &lt;- ggtree(tree, layout=&quot;circular&quot;)</div><div> for (i in 1:length(cladesData$clades)){</div><div>    p &lt;- p + geom_hilight(node=cladesData[i, &#39;node&#39;], fill = cladesData[i, &#39;col&#39;], alpha = .3) +</div><div>    geom_cladelabel(node = cladesData[i,&#39;node&#39;], label = cladesData[i], </div><div>                    color = cladesData[i,&#39;col&#39;], offset = .8, align = TRUE)</div><div>}</div></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">Where I get something like this tree in attach. I would like to know if there is a way that the clades names in the left can be aligned around the tree similarly to the names in the right. If the plotting can be done in a way that the end of the words in the left is closer to the highlight while the words in the right are already good with the beginning of the word starting near the highlight. If not, is there a way that the clade names could be written in a circular format following the tree circularity?</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">Thanks for any help you can provide<br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "64bKbfr_BQAJ",
   "from": "Yu, Guangchuang",
   "author": "cec41188340e8212",
   "date": "2019-02-26T10:07:35Z",
   "subject": "Re: Plotting clade names in circular tree",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">As described in <a href=\"https://yulab-smu.github.io/treedata-book/chapter2.html#ggtree-fortify\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://yulab-smu.github.io/treedata-book/chapter2.html%23ggtree-fortify&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792482328578000&amp;usg=AOvVaw04pmylYY4uv93XgTftw6S_\">https://yulab-smu.github.io/treedata-book/chapter2.html#ggtree-fortify</a>, you can access the data used in visualization via <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">p$data</code>, which contains node <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">angle</code> in circular layout.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">So in your for loop, you can test, whether the node <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">angle</code> is in [90, 270]. If yes, the clade label will be on the left hand side, then align the label by <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">right</code>.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Try simple code like this:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">set.seed(2019-02-26)\r\nx = rtree(30)\r\nggtree(x, layout=&#39;circular&#39;) + geom_cladelabel(50, &#39;label X&#39;, hjust=&quot;right&quot;)\r\n</code></pre><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/bioc-ggtree/attach/5fffa6dca86eb/image.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"504\" height=\"535\"><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">On Tue, Feb 26, 2019 at 5:47 PM Ana Catarina Silva <a href=\"http://mailto:ana...@gmail.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://mailto:ana...@gmail.com&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792482328578000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1x7Ey7DRiz0dQzOhNm6r3T\">anacata...@gmail.com</a> wrote:</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\"></p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:small\">Hi,</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">I am currently trying to plot several clade names in a circular tree with something like this: </div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div><br></div><div>&gt; head(cladesData)</div><div>clades  node     col</div><div>capensis   5497 #000000</div><div>hemprichii 11453 #FF7F00</div><div>lemniscatus  7407 #7FC97F</div><div>patagonicus 11563 #666666</div><div>gambianus  7666 #1F78B4</div><div>argenteus 11811 #BF5B17</div></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div> p &lt;- ggtree(tree, layout=&quot;circular&quot;)</div><div> for (i in 1:length(cladesData$clades)){</div><div>    p &lt;- p + geom_hilight(node=cladesData[i, &#39;node&#39;], fill = cladesData[i, &#39;col&#39;], alpha = .3) +</div><div>    geom_cladelabel(node = cladesData[i,&#39;node&#39;], label = cladesData[i], </div><div>                    color = cladesData[i,&#39;col&#39;], offset = .8, align = TRUE)</div><div>}</div></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">Where I get something like this tree in attach. I would like to know if there is a way that the clades names in the left can be aligned around the tree similarly to the names in the right. If the plotting can be done in a way that the end of the words in the left is closer to the highlight while the words in the right are already good with the beginning of the word starting near the highlight. If not, is there a way that the clade names could be written in a circular format following the tree circularity?</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">Thanks for any help you can provide<br></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
